$b['A fox flashes a knowing sideways grin while its huge tail curls around the entire composition. Burnt orange fur transitions into magenta and deep purple shadow'] = 'Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A fox flashes a knowing sideways grin while its huge tail curls around the entire composition. Burnt orange fur transitions into magenta and deep purple shadow. Clever, stylish, mischievous. In the right panel it is on a woman, outer thigh. Create exceptional contemporary New School tattoo artwork. REALITY IS OPTIONAL. PERSONALITY IS NOT. New School is a visual language built through intentional exaggeration, powerful silhouette, dimensional color, bold variable outlines, extreme perspective, expressive anatomy, graphic clarity and enormous personality. Do not confuse New School with generic cartoons. Do not make everything cute. Do not make everything funny. Do not create childrens illustration. Do not simply enlarge the eyes of an ordinary subject. EXAGGERATE THE THING THAT MAKES THE SUBJECT AWESOME. If the subject is a dragon, exaggerate the head, jaw, horns, claws, wings, movement or scale. If the subject is a skull, exaggerate the jaw, eye sockets, fracture, depth or perspective. If the subject is an animal, exaggerate the feature that expresses its personality. If the subject is a person, exaggerate expression, perspective, fashion, movement, hair, hands or attitude. If the subject is a machine, exaggerate the wheel, engine, lens, grille, exhaust or mechanical form. Do not exaggerate everything equally. CREATE VISUAL HIERARCHY. One dominant element should control the first read. Secondary forms should support it. Use extreme foreshortening when appropriate. Allow foreground hands, claws, mouths, eyes, wheels, horns, lenses and objects to become dramatically larger than distant forms. Compress distant elements. Overlap aggressively. Make the composition feel deep rather than flat. Some artwork should feel as though it projects physically off the body. Use bold confident outlines with intentional variation in line weight. Do not outline every internal detail equally. Outer silhouettes and important forms can carry stronger lines while interior forms use controlled line hierarchy. Use rich dimensional shading. Build convincing volume even when anatomy is impossible. Forms should feel inflated, carved, stretched, compressed, twisted or projected rather than flat. Use strong highlights, reflected color and deep shadows to create dimensionality. COLOR SHOULD FEEL ELECTRIC. Possible palettes include: Crimson and violet. Turquoise and orange. Hot pink and black. Emerald and purple. Cobalt and acid yellow. Burgundy and turquoise. Deep red and toxic green. Magenta and electric blue. Coral and violet. Gold and black. Do not use every available color simply because New School supports bright color. A sophisticated three-color palette can be more powerful than a rainbow. Use black strategically to anchor saturation. Color should create hierarchy, volume, mood and separation. New School can be: Beautiful. Dark. Sexy. Aggressive. Funny. Grotesque. Glamorous. Horrific. Psychedelic. Futuristic. Street influenced. Mythological. Surreal. Mechanical. Emotional. Epic. Do not default to comedy. Dark subjects should be allowed to remain genuinely dark. Beautiful subjects should be allowed to remain genuinely beautiful. Grotesque subjects can be disturbing without becoming childish. The subject can be a