aggerate the wheel, engine, lens, grille, exhaust or mechanical form. Do not exaggerate everything equally. CREATE VISUAL HIERARCHY. One dominant element should control the first read. Secondary forms should support it. Use extreme foreshortening when appropriate. Allow foreground hands, claws, mouths, eyes, wheels, horns, lenses and objects to become dramatically larger than distant forms. Compress distant elements. Overlap aggressively. Make the composition feel deep rather than flat. Some artwork should feel as though it projects physically off the body. Use bold confident outlines with intentional variation in line weight. Do not outline every internal detail equally. Outer silhouettes and important forms can carry stronger lines while interior forms use controlled line hierarchy. Use rich dimensional shading. Build convincing volume even when anatomy is impossible. Forms should feel inflated, carved, stretched, compressed, twisted or projected rather than flat. Use strong highlights, reflected color and deep shadows to create dimensionality. COLOR SHOULD FEEL ELECTRIC. Possible palettes include: Crimson and violet. Turquoise and orange. Hot pink and black. Emerald and purple. Cobalt and acid yellow. Burgundy and turquoise. Deep red and toxic green. Magenta and electric blue. Coral and violet. Gold and black. Do not use every available color simply because New School supports bright color. A sophisticated three-color palette can be more powerful than a rainbow. Use black strategically to anchor saturation. Color should create hierarchy, volume, mood and separation. New School can be: Beautiful. Dark. Sexy. Aggressive. Funny. Grotesque. Glamorous. Horrific. Psychedelic. Futuristic. Street influenced. Mythological. Surreal. Mechanical. Emotional. Epic. Do not default to comedy. Dark subjects should be allowed to remain genuinely dark. Beautiful subjects should be allowed to remain genuinely beautiful. Grotesque subjects can be disturbing without becoming childish. The subject can be anything. Animals. People. Women. Men. Faces. Hands. Dragons. Skulls. Skeletons. Demons. Angels. Monsters. Dark fantasy. Horror. Mythology. Cars. Motorcycles. Machines. Fashion. Street culture. Architecture. Nature. Sea life. Birds. Insects. Objects. Food. Space. Surreal worlds. Impossible creatures. Abstract concepts. NEW SCHOOL IS THE STYLE. THE SUBJECT IS NOT THE STYLE. Do not repeat the same smiling animals, giant eyeballs, food characters or cartoon skulls simply because they are common New School imagery. Push artistic ambition. The body must influence the composition. THE BODY CAN BECOME PART OF THE PERSPECTIVE. Use shoulder curvature to increase volume. Use forearm length for approaching or receding forms. Use thighs for extreme depth and large characters. Use calves for vertical movement. Use knees and elbows as rotational centers when appropriate. Use ribs for wrapping forms. Use the chest for aggressive foreground scale. Use the back for rare world-building compositions. Do not simply place rectangular illustrations onto the body. Allow creatures and objects to wrap, project, turn, disappear and reappear with the anatomy. Most tattoos should remain realistic projects for actual tattoo clients. Use small and medium pieces throughout the collection. Use large pieces when the idea requires greater depth. Reserve full sleeves, backs and chest projects for exceptional concepts. Avoid visual clutter. New School can contain tremendous information, but the primary silhouette should still read from a distance. The viewer should understand the dominant subject before discovering secondary details. Avoid generic cartoon rendering. Avoid random rainbow palettes. Avoid identical oversized eyes. Avoid endless goofy facial expressions. Avoid childish clip art. Avoid making every animal smile. Avoid making every skull funny. Avoid flat compositions. Avoid equal line weight everywhere. Avoid using graffiti merely as meaningless background. Avoid using color without hierarchy. Avoid confusing New School with Neo-Traditional. NEO-TRADITIONAL generally preserves more believable proportion, decorative illustration and controlled dimensionality. NEW SCHOOL deliberately pushes proportion, perspective, expression, volume and anatomy beyond reality. NEW SCHOOL CAN BREAK REALITY WHILE REMAINING VISUALLY COHERENT. BIG SHAPES. BIG COLOR. BIG DEPTH. BIG PERSONALITY. DARK WHEN IT WANTS TO BE. BEAUTIFUL WHEN IT WANTS TO BE. WEIRD WHEN IT WANTS TO BE. THE STYLE STAYS NEW SCHOOL. THE IMAGINATION HAS NO SPEED LIMIT'
